<article class="env-variant" id="05BM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05BM">Variant <span data-tag="05BM">4.8.6.2</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a morphism of simplicial sets. </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
We say that $F$ is a <em>$0$-categorical inner fibration</em> if, for every morphism $\Delta ^{m} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the fiber product $\Delta ^ m \times _{ \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is isomorphic to the nerve of a partially ordered set. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $F$ is a <em>$(-1)$-categorical inner fibration</em> if it induces an isomorphism from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to a full simplicial subset of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ (Definition <a data-tag="01CU" href="/tag/01CU">4.1.2.15</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For $n \leq -2$, we say that $F$ is an <em>$n$-categorical inner fibration</em> if it is an isomorphism of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
